# Behavior differences between upstream-master and main

Comparison as of VideoLAN `upstream-master` at `811ce97` and fork `main` at `090f60d`.

| Area | upstream-master | main | Why it differs | C API impact |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| CPXM cache identity | Lookups use a device-only key. | Lookups use both device and inode, so distinct files on the same filesystem do not share a cached context. | Files on the same filesystem share a device ID. Including the inode prevents one file from reusing another file’s cached decryption context. | `dvdcpxm_init(handle, NULL)` can return `-1` when only a different file on the same filesystem has a cached context. |
| Scatter buffer layout | The API documents sector-sized entries. Read paths lack per-entry alignment checks. | CSS and CPXM vectored reads reject an entry whose length is not a multiple of 2,048 before I/O, even when the total is aligned. Null bases are valid only in empty entries. | Decryption processes each entry in whole sectors. Per-entry alignment keeps a sector from extending past its buffer. | `dvdcss_readv` and `dvdcpxm_readv` return `-1` before I/O for misaligned entries. Calls that meet the documented layout are unaffected by this check. |
| Stream callback byte counts | Over-reported counts lack an explicit rejection. A short scalar read seeks to the number of blocks read as an absolute position. Vectored reads round down without seeking. | Over-reported counts return `-1`. Short scalar reads and vectored reads ending mid-sector seek to the original position plus the number of complete blocks. | A callback cannot supply more usable bytes than requested. Seeking relative to the starting position keeps the next read aligned with the actual stream offset. | Stream-backed reads return `-1` for over-reported byte counts. Short scalar reads and vectored reads ending mid-sector can invoke the seek callback at the corrected offset and fail if repositioning fails. |
